Without `router-id-neglect`, the iBGP route wins at step 9 (lower Router-ID). With `router-id-neglect`, the eBGP route is selected if it wins earlier steps (e.g., lower MED).

### Alternative Approach (if applicable):
If the goal is to prefer eBGP routes **regardless of other attributes**, set a higher **LOCAL_PREF** for eBGP routes using a route-policy:
```bash
route-policy PREFER_eBGP permit node 10
  apply local-preference 200  # Higher than default 100
bgp {as_number}
  ipv4-family vpnv4
    peer {ebgp_neighbor} route-policy PREFER_eBGP import
```

### Conclusion:
Use **`bestroute router-id-neglect`** in the BGP VPNv4 address family to allow sub-optimal eBGP routes to be selected when an iBGP path exists. This modifies the BGP best-path algorithm to skip Router-ID/Originator-ID comparisons, enabling eBGP routes to compete fairly.
